                "Consider a high-stakes statutory auditing dilemma: The external audit engagement team completed all substantive fieldwork and subsequent events review on February 20, 2026. The engagement partner and audit team dated the statutory audit report February 20, 2026, marking the formal close of the auditor's active subsequent events testing window. However, before the financial statements and audit report are published and filed with the SEC on March 5, 2026, a material subsequent event occurs on February 28: a catastrophic explosion destroys the company's flagship semiconductor fabrication facility, causing $80M in unrecoverable damages. Because this event arose before issuance, it must be disclosed in the financial statements under ASC 855 / ISA 560.",
                "The Auditor's Procedural Crossroads: When a material subsequent event occurs after the date of the auditor's report but before the financial statements are issued, the statutory auditor faces two distinct regulatory options under PCAOB AS 3110 (Dating of the Independent Auditor's Report) and ISA 560 paragraphs 12-13: Option A: Single-Dating (Advancing the Entire Audit Report Date): The auditor extends the date of the entire audit report to the date of the subsequent event (or the date fieldwork on the event was completed), such as March 2, 2026. However, advancing the overall audit report date carries enormous legal and liability implications! Under professional standards, if the auditor advances the report date to March 2, the auditor's active statutory responsibility for detecting ALL subsequent events across ALL operational cycles is extended from February 20 to March 2! The audit team would be legally required to perform full subsequent events fieldwork across the entire enterprise up to March 2, including reading new board minutes, reviewing latest cash transactions, and obtaining refreshed legal updates.",
